Giardiasis
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Foul-smelling greasy diarrhoea, Profound flatulence/sulphur burps, History of untreated water consumption, Flagellated trophozoites in stool

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-010
Reason for Visit: Prolonged, worsening abdominal distension, severe crampy pain, and unremitting malodorous diarrhoea requiring urgent assessment to rule out bowel obstruction or severe malabsorption.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with profound, severe abdominal bloating, significant weight loss, and intractable vague periumbilical pain. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate the acute abdomen, rule out mechanical obstruction or acute biliary pathology, and manage profound dehydration prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is incredibly swollen, the cramps are constant, and I can't stop burping up sulphur."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 3 weeks ago following a backpacking trip where the patient consumed untreated stream water. Symptoms have progressively worsened, characterized by prolonged, recurrent, and chronic watery diarrhoea that has recently become greasy, frothy, and highly malodorous. The patient describes profound, severe abdominal bloating, flatulence, and significant distension. A key feature is frequent, foul-smelling belching described exactly as tasting like sulphur or rotten eggs. Accompanied by crampy, vague periumbilical abdominal pain, profound anorexia, significant nausea, and prominent fatigue. Patient notes an unintentional weight loss of 5kg. Patient reports a complete absence of macroscopic blood or significant pus in the stool. Previously refractory to simple, short-course empiric over-the-counter antidiarrheal therapies.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but severely fatigued; distressed by abdominal distension.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; progressive weakness and weight loss are concerning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg (down from baseline 70 Kg)
General: Pale, appearing chronically unwell and fatigued, frequently shifting position due to abdominal discomfort.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is markedly distended and tympanic to percussion throughout. Diffuse, vague periumbilical tenderness on deep palpation, but no rebound, guarding, or rigidity. Bowel sounds are hyperactive and borborygmi are audible without a stethoscope. Digital rectal exam deferred. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Foul-smelling greasy diarrhoea, Profound flatulence/sulphur burps, History of untreated water consumption, and Flagellated trophozoites in stool (pending laboratory confirmation).
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Various empty over-the-counter antidiarrheal packets visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter loperamide with no clinical improvement.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Giardiasis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of ongoing profuse diarrhoea (rather than absolute obstipation) and the specific nature of the sulphur belching.

Coeliac Disease flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute onset chronologically linked to a specific environmental exposure (stream water), though clinical symptoms of malabsorption overlap.

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The predominance of lower gastrointestinal symptoms (frothy diarrhoea, severe flatulence) rather than localized epigastric pain responding to antacids.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with profound malabsorptive diarrhoea, significant unintentional weight loss, and marked tympanic abdominal distension. The severity of the bloating and progressive vague abdominal pain raised a legitimate clinical concern for a subacute bowel obstruction or severe evolving intra-abdominal pathology. A direct physical examination was strictly necessary to rule out an acute surgical abdomen and assess the degree of chronic hypovolemia. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of delayed diagnosis of a potential obstruction, fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cease all over-the-counter antidiarrheals. Prescribe empirical oral Tinidazole (2g as a single dose) or Metronidazole. Request urgent stool microscopy for cysts and trophozoites (looking for the 'owl-face' appearance), alongside a multiplex PCR. Advise strict adherence to a lactose-free diet.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly eliminating all dairy products from the diet for the next 4 weeks, the patient will prevent the severe, explosive secondary lactase-deficiency symptoms; the rapid reduction in bloating will serve as powerful positive reinforcement to maintain the dietary restriction.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 72 hours to ensure eradication and symptom resolution.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient develops sudden, severe, and constant abdominal pain, board-like abdominal rigidity, bilious (green) vomiting, or absolute cessation of bowel movements and flatus.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending stool microscopy; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Symptoms should rapidly improve within 48 to 72 hours of targeted antibiotic therapy, though secondary lactose intolerance may persist for several weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The parasite damages the lining of your gut, making you temporarily unable to digest milk. Seek immediate emergency care if your stomach pain changes from crampy to constant and agonizing, or if you start vomiting green fluid.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Standard antidiarrheals fail because this is a resilient protozoan parasite, not a simple bacteria. It coats the inside of your intestine, physically blocking nutrient absorption, which causes the greasy stools and massive gas production.
Actionable Steps: Take the prescribed antibiotic exactly as directed. Do not consume any alcohol during treatment and for 3 days after, as it will cause violent vomiting. Avoid all dairy products.

